You are the skeptic. A reviewer claims this defect in the range above:
TITLE: A mark is dropped unless the mouse is released inside the same text div: overshoot past a choice, into the next row, or a stem-to-choice drag marks nothing
FILE: src/features/rc/components/RcChoiceRow.tsx:201
SEVERITY CLAIMED: major
SCENARIO: Yellow marker active. (a) Drag from the start of choice B's text and release on choice C's row (or in B's own li padding / over the eye column / in the gap below B): onAddHighlight is never called and the blue selection is left standing, so the student sees selected text with no highlight. (b) Drag from the stem into choice A and release on A: nothing is marked in either surface. All three cases proven with vitest against RcQuestionPane: onAdd not called; a control drag released inside B calls onAdd('q42:B', 0, 11, ...). LR's OfficialQuestionPane handles all of these because it listens on the whole pane and clips the selection to each region; the RC comment in the LR commit ('a drag spanning stem into a choice marks both') does not hold for RC.
EVIDENCE: RcChoiceRow: <div ... data-rc-mark-surface onMouseUp={surface.onMouseUp} onClick={clickText}>  // mouseup only on the text box
rcHighlightPaint.captureRenderedSelection: if (!container.contains(range.commonAncestorContainer)) return null;  // any range that crosses the surface edge is discarded
RcQuestionPane: onMouseUp={dismissOutsideSurfaces}  // a release on the li/eye/next row only closes the popover
PROPOSED FIX: Move the mouse-up ladder to the pane (as LR's handleMouseUp does): on pane mouseup, for each surface (stem + choices) intersect the live Range with that surface's text (Range.compareBoundaryPoints / cloneRange clipped to the container) and call that surface's onAdd/onRemoveRange with the clipped offsets. At minimum, make captureRenderedSelection clip the range to the container instead of returning null when commonAncestorContainer is outside it, and attach the surface's onMouseUp on the li rather than the text div.

Try to REFUTE it. Read the actual code at that location and everything it depends on. If the scenario cannot happen, or is pre-existing behaviour unchanged by this range, or is already handled elsewhere, say real=false and explain precisely. If it is real, confirm with the exact code path and rate its true severity. Default to real=false when uncertain. Return the verdict.

